WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.800
Clustering or Cluster Analysis is one of the&nbsp;
foundational and most important tasks that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:16.320
can do in unsupervised machine learning. The&nbsp;
general idea is to take an entire collection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:22.240
of cases or observations and put them together.&nbsp;
Now the important thing to remember is these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.840
not natural groupings, rather these are Groups&nbsp;
of Convenience. They're not the platonic real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.440
universals that cut nature on its joints. They're&nbsp;
groups of cases that are similar to each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:38.560
but with a pragmatic goal. You group the&nbsp;
cases to accomplish a specific purpose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.800
And the real merit of the grouping is how well&nbsp;
does it serve that purpose. You can think of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.400
as a version of Functionalism. And the question&nbsp;
is, which cases can be treated in the same way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.960
depending on a specific purpose?
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.800
Clustering serves really important&nbsp;
purposes in a lot of different fields.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.640
To give you a few examples, in Marketing the idea&nbsp;
here is that you make offers to people who are in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:09.200
similar categories. You give them the same kind&nbsp;
of ads. You recommend the same products to them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.840
It's extremely common in Marketing. In Medicine,&nbsp;
you would use clustering to identify similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.360
patients or cases or outbreaks or even medications&nbsp;
that can be used for similar kinds of diseases and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:28.160
disorders. In Ecology and Zoology, you can use it&nbsp;
to identify species and ways of categorizing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.320
environments that they live in.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.680
Although it's not formally used in Law, the basic&nbsp;
idea here is you're trying to argue that one case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:41.360
is similar to another. If you wanted to do a&nbsp;
quantitative analysis, you could establish that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.160
for this particular purpose, this case, is&nbsp;
in the same category as all of these others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:51.600
and they go together and they're different from&nbsp;
these ones. That's what Cluster Analysis is about.&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:59.120
Note that clusters are pragmatic groupings. They&nbsp;
really serve a particular purpose for the analyst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.640
or the purpose using them. What clusters do is&nbsp;
they allow limited interchangeability between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:10.640
the objects within the cluster. You want to hear&nbsp;
these songs together. You’re going to give the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:17.200
same ads to this group over here. These people get&nbsp;
the same medical treatment. And the approach is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.000
the validity of clustering varies according to&nbsp;
the individual person's purpose, the data that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.160
fed into the system and the algorithm used.
The algorithm defines what it means to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.560
similar and it also defines how to measure&nbsp;
distance and what it means to be a cluster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.160
There are many choices available. They&nbsp;
fall into a few general categories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.680
For instance, there are methods that measure the&nbsp;
distance between points like euclidean distance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.720
There are others that measure distance from&nbsp;
a centroid, others that look at the density&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.240
of the data in the space and others that use&nbsp;
distributional models. If you have graph data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:00.560
that is network data, there are other models too.
Let's start by taking a quick look at distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.680
algorithms. Imagine you have a collection&nbsp;
of points here in two dimensional space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.560
and that's one cluster in orange and you've&nbsp;
got another cluster here in blue. What you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.560
going to do is you're going to measure the&nbsp;
distance from every point to every other point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.760
Think of it as measuring the direct distance&nbsp;
between each of them and these are also known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:25.440
as connectivity models. One of the neat things&nbsp;
you get from these is a hierarchical diagram.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:31.920
One of the interesting choices here is whether you&nbsp;
want to do a joining or splitting approach. Those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.440
are actually technically known as agglomerative&nbsp;
or divisive. You either start with all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.480
data in one cluster and then take it apart or&nbsp;
you start with all observations separately and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:48.160
then join them together sequentially. One of the&nbsp;
tricks here is that, these distance models really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.920
only find convex clusters. That is if you're in&nbsp;
a two dimensional or three dimensional shape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.880
The other major limitation is that while&nbsp;
the distance models are easy to describe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.200
they're really slow for big data because you&nbsp;
have to compute a big number of distances.&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.320
The next most common methods are the centroid&nbsp;
based models. Imagine that you have cluster of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.040
points here and what we get with this diamond is&nbsp;
a point that defines the mean in multi-dimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.080
space here. What it does then is it looks at&nbsp;
the distance from every point in a group to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:26.160
centroid. The center point here, the centroid,&nbsp;
is defined by a mean vector, so it's the mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:31.520
for that group on every variable that you have in&nbsp;
the dimensional space. The most common version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:36.560
this is what's called k-means and that's where you&nbsp;
simply define how many centroids you want to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:40.320
and then it figures out how far each&nbsp;
point is from so many different centroids.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.720
Like the distance models, the centroid model finds&nbsp;
convex clusters but with an extra limitation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:53.680
They're generally of similar size. The other major&nbsp;
problem is you have to choose the value for k,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:57.600
the number of clusters that you want&nbsp;
to have. There are some interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.080
adaptation sways around that but generally,&nbsp;
you just kind of pick a number and you give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:08.640
that to the computer and see how well it works.
Next, there are density algorithms and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.440
where you look in the multi-dimensional space and&nbsp;
it simply computes the density of points in there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.880
and it draws a border around them so for instance.&nbsp;
What you're doing here is you're connecting dense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:23.840
regions in k-dimensional space where k is the&nbsp;
number of variables or attributes that you have.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:28.640
What's neat about this is, these&nbsp;
approaches can model non-convex clusters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:35.520
You see for instance how the orange one bends in.&nbsp;
It can also model clusters of different sizes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.360
The blue one's smaller, the orange one's bigger&nbsp;
and so it overcomes some of the limitations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.800
other approaches. One interesting thing is,&nbsp;
it is possible to sort of ignore outliers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:52.000
or unusual cases because they're not in&nbsp;
dense regions. The major problem however&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.000
is that it's hard to describe exactly how&nbsp;
the algorithm arrived at these things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:01.440
You don't have a parametric distribution to say&nbsp;
that this cluster's defined by high scores on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:06.320
this variable and low scores on that one because&nbsp;
it might be an unusual shape that wraps around.&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.160
The fourth collection of algorithms are&nbsp;
distributions or distributional models.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:18.160
Imagine we have our points here and we draw a sort&nbsp;
of normal shape around them in a bivariate model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:23.840
that's going to look like an ellipse. Here we draw&nbsp;
an approximate ellipse around these points so what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:28.480
we're doing here is we're getting the clusters and&nbsp;
we're modeling them as statistical distributions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.080
Now, you have a lot of choices but by far&nbsp;
the most common is a multivariate normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:37.600
distribution and we’re showing that&nbsp;
with the variate loose ellipses here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:44.880
There's one major problem to the distributional&nbsp;
approach is that, it is prone to overfit. That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:49.760
if you give the computer free rein, it'll just&nbsp;
add more and more dimensions until you end up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:55.520
models that really only apply to the exact data&nbsp;
that you currently have. Now, you can overcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:00.160
that by telling it how many dimensions to work&nbsp;
with, similar to the k-means, and tell it how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:06.000
many clusters. One advantage though is, it's good&nbsp;
at capturing the correlations or interrelations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:11.760
between the attributes in your data, which none of&nbsp;
the other approaches do specifically. On the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.480
hand, it also is limited to convex clusters,&nbsp;
because that's the shape of our distributions.&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:23.520
In summary, there are multiple definitions of&nbsp;
clusters. There's definitions of what it means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:28.800
to be a cluster, how do they define distance&nbsp;
and how to define shape. While there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:35.120
several categories of algorithms, each of them has&nbsp;
different strengths and limitations. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:41.040
some only do convex shapes, some do equal size&nbsp;
clusters, some are difficult for large data sets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:46.400
some are hard to describe parametrically. So,&nbsp;
when it comes down to actually conducting your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:51.280
cluster analysis, you need to be mindful of&nbsp;
these options and try to choose an algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:57.840
that both fits your data and fits the purposes&nbsp;
or pragmatic applications of your analysis.

